            <ArticleTitle>Functioning uterus with absent cervix, short vagina and transverse septum.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>386-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">06.2009/JCPSP.386388</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 25-year-old married lady with primary amenorrhea, infertility, lower abdominal pain and dyspareunia underwent vaginoplasty, total abdominal hysterectomy with right adnexal clearance for congenital absent cervix, vaginal septum with functioning uterus and right sided endometrioma. Mould was kept in vagina for 2 weeks followed by intermittent vaginal dilatation for one week. Couple was advised normal coital function and intermittent vaginal dilatation at home. Patient was followed regularly in OPD for 4 months and there was no complaint regarding sexual life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
